    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "The full suite (a single acceptance test class) passes.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe spec required making `POST /api/owners` idempotent: a repeat carrying an already-seen `Idempotency-Key` returns the originally created owner with **200** instead of creating a duplicate (which otherwise 409s).\n\n**Approach \u2014 minimize structural impact.** The `addOwner` method lives in `OwnerRestControllerV1`, a large class with very high `WMC_other` and an already-branchy `addOwner` (high CC). Adding idempotency logic there would multiply changed-lines \u00d7 CC \u00d7 the whole class's complexity. Instead, I added the behaviour as a single small, cohesive unit that **touches no existing function**:\n\n- **New file:** `src/main/java/.../rest/IdempotentOwnerCreateFilter.java` \u2014 a `OncePerRequestFilter` (`@Component`, auto-registered) with one method. On `POST \u2026/api/owners` carrying an `Idempotency-Key`:\n  - if the key was seen, it replays the stored create body with status 200;\n  - otherwise it captures the response via `ContentCachingResponseWrapper`, and stores the body only when the create returned 201.\n\nThis keeps the controller untouched (its per-function cost stays 0), touches exactly one file, and the only changed function is a fresh method in a near-empty class (minimal `WMC_other`). Matching on `endsWith(\"/api/owners\")` is robust to the configured `/petclinic/` context path and won't match the nested pet/visit POSTs.",
